in syndrome manifests through a constellation of symptoms including myoclonus, hyperreflexia, muscle rigidity, diaphoresis, hypotension, and potentially coma. These arise from excessive serotonergic activity in the central nervous system. Clinical descriptions emphasize that the syndrome can be fatal, with reported features such as agitation, hyperthermia, tachycardia, and altered mental status [Data: Entities (15162); Relationships (7652, 7648, 17564, 17563)]. Specifically, hyperreflexia and myoclonus are consistently cited as hallmark signs in both human cases and animal models [Data: Relationships (7648, 7652)]. Additionally, co-administration of serotonergic agents like fluoxetine and tryptophan has been linked to lethal outcomes involving rigidity, diaphoresis, and coma [Data: Relationships (17564, 17563)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Coma is a critical endpoint shared by multiple severe adverse reactions\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Coma emerges as a high-severity symptom linked to both lithium toxicity and serotonin syndrome, underscoring its role as a marker of life-threatening drug toxicity. It is also contraindicated with certain herbal agents like Bai Shao (white peony root), which has central nervous system depressant effects [Data: Entities (18167); Relationships (18674, 17563, 6836)]. In lithium toxicity, coma occurs in advanced stages and may result from delayed diagnosis or drug interactions [Data: Relationships (18674)]. Similarly, serotonin syndrome can progress to coma, especially when multiple serotonergic drugs are combined [Data: Relationships (17563)]. The high degree score (10) for coma among symptom entities further highlights its centrality in severe adverse events [Data: Entities (18167)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Tricyclic antidepressants and benzodiazepines contribute to cognitive and motor impairment\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) and benzodiazepines like alprazolam are linked to adverse reactions that impair cognition and motor function. TCAs are associated with dysphagia, drowsiness, and, in toxic states, coma and confusion [Data: Relationships (8068, 8128)]. Alprazolam use can lead to persistent dysarthria, somnolence, and ataxia [Data: Relationships (16985)]. Additionally, phenobarbital sodium—a barbiturate—is explicitly noted to cause cognitive impairment, especially with prolonged use [Data: Relationships (15841)]. These findings suggest that commonly prescribed psychotropic medications carry significant risks for neurological side effects that overlap with those seen in toxic syndromes like lithium poisoning and serotonin excess.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Neurological symptoms such as myoclonus and hyperreflexia serve as diagnostic indicators\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Myoclonus and hyperreflexia are recurrent symptoms strongly associated with serotonin syndrome, appearing in both clinical reports and experimental models. These signs reflect central nervous system hyperexcitability due to excessive serotonin activity [Data: Relationships (7652, 7648)]. Myoclonus is described alongside seizures and fever in animal studies, and these effects can be antagonized by serotonin inhibitors, confirming the mechanistic link [Data: Relationships (7652)]. Hyperreflexia is similarly highlighted as a core feature in human cases of serotonin syndrome, often co-occurring with rigidity and autonomic instability [Data: Relationships (7648, 17564)]. Their presence should prompt immediate evaluation for serotonergic drug toxicity.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Autonomic symptoms like diaphoresis and extreme thirst signal systemic toxicity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Profuse sweating (diaphoresis) and extreme thirst are prominent autonomic symptoms tied to both lithium toxicity and serotonin syndrome. Diaphoresis appears in descriptions of serotonin syn